=== lesson
id: ds-linked
title: Linked lists
summary: A linked list keeps each item in its own node, anywhere in memory, and links every node to the next one. Getting to item i means following i links, but adding or removing at the front, or next to a node you're holding, only changes a link or two. You'll build and walk linked lists, compare them with arrays operation by operation, and meet the three kinds you'll study next: singly, doubly and circular.

=== learn
### Nodes and links
An array keeps its items side by side in one block. A **linked list** doesn't: each item lives in its own small object, a **node**, which can be anywhere in memory. Each node holds its item and a **link** to the next node: a reference that says where the next node is. The list only has to remember where the first node is, which is called the **head**. The last node's link is `None`: that marks the end.

| Node at address | Holds | Link to the next node |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2040 | 17 | 1100 |
| 1100 | 42 | 3300 |
| 3300 | 8 | None |

The nodes are scattered (2040, 1100, 3300), but following the links from the head visits them in order: 17, then 42, then 8. You met the node class in the Classes lesson:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(17, Node(42, Node(8)))
print(head.val, head.next.val, head.next.next.val)
print(head.next.next.next)
```
```output
17 42 8
None
```
Python makes `Node(8)` first (the innermost call), then the node holding 42, which links to it, then the node holding 17. `head` is the first node, and every other node is reached from it.

### Walking the list
There's no formula for where item i is, because the nodes aren't evenly spaced. The only way to reach a node is to start at the head and follow the links, one at a time:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def get(head, i):
    node = head
    for step in range(i):
        node = node.next
    return node.val

def to_list(head):
    items = []
    node = head
    while node is not None:
        items.append(node.val)
        node = node.next
    return items

head = Node(17, Node(42, Node(8)))
print(get(head, 2))
print(to_list(head))
```
```output
8
[17, 42, 8]
```
`get(head, 2)` follows 2 links. Reaching item i takes i steps: O(i), and O(n) for the last item, where an array needs one step, O(1). `to_list` visits every node, O(n), and is a handy way to print a linked list. Animation 1 walks to an item.

### Inserting at the front: O(1)
Adding an item at the front of an array shifts every item one place right: O(n). In a linked list nothing moves. Make a new node that links to the old head, and make the new node the head:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(42, Node(8))
head = Node(17, head)
print(head.val, head.next.val, head.next.next.val)
```
```output
17 42 8
```
`Node(17, head)` makes a node that links to the old first node, 42. Then the assignment to `head` moves the head to the new node. That's the same two steps however long the list is: O(1). Removing the first node is O(1) too: `head = head.next` moves the head past it.

### Inserting and deleting after a node: O(1)
When you're already holding a node, you can insert a new node right after it by changing two links. Nothing else moves:

1. `new = Node(99, node.next)`: the new node links to the node that used to come after `node`.
2. `node.next = new`: `node` now links to the new node.

The order matters. If `node.next = new` ran first, the link to the rest of the list would be overwritten before the new node had copied it, and the rest of the list would be lost. To **delete** the node after `node`, link around it: `node.next = node.next.next`. Nothing links to the skipped node any more, so Python frees its memory.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def to_list(head):
    items = []
    node = head
    while node is not None:
        items.append(node.val)
        node = node.next
    return items

head = Node(17, Node(42, Node(8)))
node = head.next
new = Node(99, node.next)
node.next = new
print(to_list(head))
head.next = head.next.next
print(to_list(head))
```
```output
[17, 42, 99, 8]
[17, 99, 8]
```
The link changes are O(1). But to **get to** the node at index i you first walk i links, so inserting at index i costs O(i) for the walk and O(1) for the links. A linked list is fast at changing things where you already are, and slow at getting somewhere. Animation 2 shows an insert.

### Arrays and linked lists compared

| Operation | Array (Python `list`) | Linked list |
| --- | --- | --- |
| read or write item i | O(1) | O(i): walk from the head |
| insert or delete at the front | O(n): every item shifts | O(1): change the head |
| insert or delete next to a node you hold | O(n): the items after it shift | O(1): change one or two links |
| add at the end | O(1) amortized | O(n) to walk there, or O(1) if you also keep a `tail` link to the last node |
| search for a value | O(n), or O(log n) with binary search if sorted | O(n), even if sorted: there's no jumping to the middle |
| extra memory | some spare capacity | one link (or two) for every item |

Two more differences matter in practice. An array's items sit side by side, which a computer reads very fast, while a linked list's nodes are scattered, so walking a linked list is slower even though both walks are O(n). And a linked list never has to copy all its items to grow: each new node is a small separate piece of memory.

### Three kinds of linked list
The next three lessons look at each kind in depth:

| Kind | Each node has | Where it ends | Walk backwards? | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **singly linked** | `val`, `next` | the last node's `next` is None | no | stacks, hash map buckets, simple lists |
| **doubly linked** | `val`, `prev`, `next` | the first node's `prev` and the last node's `next` are None | yes, along `prev` | browser history, undo and redo, caches |
| **circular** | `val`, `next` (and `prev`, if it's doubly linked) | it doesn't: the last node links back to the first | only if doubly linked | taking turns, playlists on repeat |

A circular list has no None at the end, so a walk round it never stops by itself. To go round once, remember the node you started from and stop when you're back at it. `node is start` asks whether `node` and `start` are the very same node:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

a = Node("Ann")
b = Node("Ben")
c = Node("Cy")
a.next = b
b.next = c
c.next = a

node = a
for turn in range(5):
    print(node.val, end=" ")
    node = node.next
print()

start = b
node = start
while True:
    print(node.val, end=" ")
    node = node.next
    if node is start:
        break
print()
```
```output
Ann Ben Cy Ann Ben
Ben Cy Ann
```
The first loop shows the turns going round and round: after Cy comes Ann again, with no end to fall off. The second loop goes round exactly once: it starts at Ben and stops as soon as `node is start`, back at Ben.

### What the extra link buys
A doubly linked node knows both of its neighbours, so a doubly linked list can do two things quickly that a singly linked list can't. Both columns assume the list keeps a `head` and a `tail`:

| Operation | Singly linked | Doubly linked |
| --- | --- | --- |
| insert or delete at the front | O(1) | O(1) |
| insert at the end | O(1) | O(1) |
| delete the last node | O(n): walk to find the node before it | O(1): it's `tail.prev` |
| delete a node you hold | O(n): walk to find the node before it | O(1): its `prev` and `next` are both known |
| walk backwards | not possible | O(n), along `prev` |
| links per node | 1 | 2 |

Deleting a node means changing the link of the node **before** it. A singly linked node doesn't know which node that is, so you have to walk from the head to find it. A doubly linked node has it in `prev`. The price is a second link in every node, and twice as many links to keep right on every insert and delete. A circular list costs the same as the singly or doubly linked list it's made from; what it adds is a way back to the start.

### When a linked list beats an array
A linked list is the better choice when you:

- insert and delete at the front a lot, as in a stack or a queue,
- insert and delete at a place you're already holding, like the cursor of a text editor or the song that's playing in a playlist,
- never need item i by its index.

An array is the better choice when you:

- read items by index, or use binary search,
- mostly walk through the items in order, which is faster when they sit side by side,
- want to use as little memory as possible per item.

In Python, `list` is an array, and you'll rarely need a hand-made linked list for everyday programs. But linked nodes are the building block of the stacks, queues, hash maps, trees and graphs in the rest of this unit, and Python's `collections.deque`, coming up in the Queues lesson, is built from linked blocks.

### Solving problems with linked lists
The rest of this lesson is about solving problems with linked lists, the way the tasks do. Five patterns cover the tasks, and a clue in the question usually tells you which one you need:

| Pattern | Clue in the question |
| --- | --- |
| Walking with a pointer | counting, adding up, searching, or comparing each node with its neighbour |
| Building at the front | an answer that comes out backwards, like the digits of a number |
| Changing the links after a node | removing, inserting or splicing nodes in the middle of a list |
| Building in order with a tail | a new list built from the start to the end, or nodes sorted into several lists |
| Copying the values into a list | item i, its mirror n − 1 − i, or going backwards |

After the patterns come the combinations that need two of them, and then two steps for the second part of every task: edge cases, and time and space.

In the tasks, the starter code has the `Node` class and two helpers: `from_list(values)` builds a linked list from a Python list and returns its head, and `to_list(head)` walks a linked list and returns its values as a Python list. Each task also has a function `run` that the tests call: it builds the linked lists your function needs, calls it, and turns the answer back into a Python list. So `run([1, 1, 2])` means "your function gets the list 1 → 1 → 2". Keep `run` and the helpers as they are.

### Walking with a pointer
Almost every linked list task starts with a walk: a pointer, `node`, starts at the head and moves along with `node = node.next` until it's None. While it walks, keep whatever the task needs: a count, a total, the best so far, or the position, which goes up by 1 at each step. Reading a number whose digits are the nodes works the same way: each step multiplies what you have by the base and adds the new digit.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(1, Node(0, Node(1, Node(1))))
count = 0
number = 0
node = head
while node is not None:
    count += 1
    number = number * 2 + node.val
    node = node.next
print(count, number)
```
```output
4 11
```
The bits 1, 0, 1, 1 make the binary number 1011, which is 11.

To compare each node with the one before it, keep a second pointer, `prev`, one step behind. (To look at the node after it instead, walk while `node.next` is not None as well, so that `node.next.val` is always there to read.)
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(4, Node(9, Node(9, Node(2))))
prev = head
node = head.next
while node is not None:
    if node.val == prev.val:
        print("repeat:", node.val)
    prev = node
    node = node.next
```
```output
repeat: 9
```
Two lists can be walked side by side, too: give each its own pointer, and move a pointer on only when its node has been used. And when you need to know whether two pointers are at the **same node**, compare them with `is`: two different nodes can hold the same value, and `==` on their values can't tell them apart.

### Building at the front
`head = Node(x, head)` adds x in front of everything, in O(1). Adding items one by one at the front leaves them in the reverse of the order they came in. That's what you want when the answer comes out backwards: the digits of a number come out last digit first, with `% 10` (or `% 2` for bits), so inserting each at the front leaves them in the right order.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

n = 13
head = Node(n % 2)
n //= 2
while n > 0:
    head = Node(n % 2, head)
    n //= 2

bits = []
node = head
while node is not None:
    bits.append(node.val)
    node = node.next
print(bits)
```
```output
[1, 1, 0, 1]
```
13 % 2 is 1, the last bit, which goes in first and ends up last.

Building at the front never changes the nodes that are already there, so the old head still leads to the same list as before. Two lists can even share their nodes from some point on: after `a = Node(1, rest)` and `b = Node(2, rest)`, both lists carry on with rest. That's safe as long as nothing changes the shared nodes, and it lets a new version of a list cost O(1) instead of a copy.

### Changing the links after a node
Inserting after a node is `node.next = Node(x, node.next)`, and deleting the node after it is `node.next = node.next.next`, both O(1). While walking, decide at each node whether to change the link after it. After a delete, **stay** where you are: the node that is now next hasn't been checked yet. After an insert, step over the new node, or you'd go on to check the node you made.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(1, Node(1, Node(2, Node(2, Node(2)))))
node = head
while node is not None and node.next is not None:
    if node.next.val == node.val:
        node.next = node.next.next
    else:
        node = node.next

values = []
node = head
while node is not None:
    values.append(node.val)
    node = node.next
print(values)
```
```output
[1, 2]
```
Cutting a list in two is the same kind of change: remember `node.next`, which starts the second part, then `node.next = None` ends the first part at node. Splicing a whole list into a gap takes two links: the node before the gap links to the new list's head, and the new list's last node links to the node after the gap.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def values(head):
    out = []
    while head is not None:
        out.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return out

head = Node(1, Node(2, Node(3, Node(4))))
node = head.next
second = node.next
node.next = None
print(values(head), values(second))
```
```output
[1, 2] [3, 4]
```

### Building in order with a tail
To build a list in order, keep two pointers: head, the first node, and tail, the last. Adding x is `tail.next = Node(x)`, then `tail = tail.next`: O(1), with no walk to the end. The first node is the special case, because there's no tail yet to link from, so check `if tail is None:` and make the new node both head and tail.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = None
tail = None
for x in [5, 7, 9]:
    node = Node(x)
    if tail is None:
        head = node
    else:
        tail.next = node
    tail = node

values = []
node = head
while node is not None:
    values.append(node.val)
    node = node.next
print(values)
```
```output
[5, 7, 9]
```
Building a list doesn't need new nodes: you can move the nodes you already have onto the end of a new list. That's how merging and splitting work without extra space. Keep a head and a tail for each list you build, and before moving a node, remember the node after it and set its `next` to None: otherwise its old link would drag the rest of the old list along with it.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def values(head):
    out = []
    while head is not None:
        out.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return out

heads = [None, None]
tails = [None, None]
node = Node(1, Node(4, Node(3, Node(2))))
while node is not None:
    nxt = node.next
    node.next = None
    side = node.val % 2
    if tails[side] is None:
        heads[side] = node
    else:
        tails[side].next = node
    tails[side] = node
    node = nxt
print(values(heads[0]), values(heads[1]))
```
```output
[4, 2] [1, 3]
```
The even values went to list 0 and the odd ones to list 1, each in its original order. Joining them is one more link: `tails[0].next = heads[1]`.

### Copying the values into a list
A walk only goes forwards. When a task needs item i straight away, the item n − 1 − i that mirrors it, or the list read backwards, copy the values into a Python list first, in one O(n) walk. After that, indexes work as usual. The price is O(n) extra space.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(3, Node(5, Node(5, Node(3))))
values = []
node = head
while node is not None:
    values.append(node.val)
    node = node.next
n = len(values)
ends = values[0] + values[n - 1]
print(ends, values == values[::-1])
```
```output
6 True
```
With many questions about positions, copying once pays off: each question is then O(1), where walking from the head every time would be O(n) per question.

### Combining patterns
Many tasks need two patterns at once:

- **Changing links as you walk** (walk + after). Walk the list, and at each node decide whether to delete, insert or cut after it. Stay put after a delete, and step over a node you've inserted.
- **Building a new list as you walk** (walk + tail). Walk one or more lists, and add each piece of the answer to the end of a new list with a tail, like the sums of the parts between markers, or the smaller of two front nodes when merging.
- **Building backwards as you walk** (walk + front). Walk a list and insert each value at the front of a new list: the new list comes out reversed, and the old one is left as it was.
- **Reading backwards from a copy** (array + front). Copy the values into a Python list, read them from the end, and build the answer at the front, so it comes out in the right order. Adding numbers whose digits start with the biggest works this way.
- **Relinking nodes onto tails** (tail + after). Move each node onto the tail of one of several new lists, cutting its old link as it goes, then link the lists one after another.

Changing links as you walk: insert a 0 between every two neighbouring nodes.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(1, Node(2, Node(3)))
node = head
while node.next is not None:
    node.next = Node(0, node.next)
    node = node.next.next

values = []
node = head
while node is not None:
    values.append(node.val)
    node = node.next
print(values)
```
```output
[1, 0, 2, 0, 3]
```
`node = node.next.next` steps over the new 0, straight to the old next node.

Building a new list as you walk: the sums of the parts between the 0s.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = None
for x in reversed([0, 3, 1, 0, 4, 0]):
    head = Node(x, head)
result = None
tail = None
total = 0
node = head.next
while node is not None:
    if node.val == 0:
        new = Node(total)
        if tail is None:
            result = new
        else:
            tail.next = new
        tail = new
        total = 0
    else:
        total += node.val
    node = node.next
print(result.val, result.next.val)
```
```output
4 4
```
Each 0 closes a part: 3 + 1 = 4, then 4.

Building backwards as you walk: a reversed copy.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(1, Node(2, Node(3)))
copy = None
node = head
while node is not None:
    copy = Node(node.val, copy)
    node = node.next
print(copy.val, copy.next.val, copy.next.next.val)
print(head.val, head.next.val, head.next.next.val)
```
```output
3 2 1
1 2 3
```
The copy has new nodes, so the original list doesn't change.

Reading backwards from a copy: 99 + 5 with the digits biggest first, where the adding has to start from the last digit.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

a = [9, 9]
b = [5]
head = None
carry = 0
i = len(a) - 1
j = len(b) - 1
while i >= 0 or j >= 0 or carry > 0:
    total = carry
    if i >= 0:
        total += a[i]
        i -= 1
    if j >= 0:
        total += b[j]
        j -= 1
    head = Node(total % 10, head)
    carry = total // 10
print(head.val, head.next.val, head.next.next.val)
```
```output
1 0 4
```
The digits come out last first (4, 0, 1) and each goes in at the front, so the list reads 1, 0, 4.

Relinking nodes onto tails: the last example of the tail step sorted nodes onto two lists without making a single new node. One more link joins them:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

small_head = Node(1, Node(2))
small_tail = small_head.next
big_head = Node(5, Node(4))
small_tail.next = big_head

values = []
node = small_head
while node is not None:
    values.append(node.val)
    node = node.next
print(values)
```
```output
[1, 2, 5, 4]
```
If the first list could be empty, check for that: then the answer is the second list's head.

### Edge cases to test
Once your code passes the examples, look for inputs that could break it: the smallest, emptiest, most extreme or most unusual inputs the problem allows. For the tasks here, try:

| Edge case | Example | What can go wrong |
| --- | --- | --- |
| an empty list | `run([])` | reading `head.val` when head is None raises `AttributeError` |
| one node | `run([7])` | a loop that needs `node.next` never runs, or runs off the end |
| the head changes | deleting or inserting before the first node | returning the old head, which is no longer first |
| the last node | a change at the very end | forgetting to end the list with None, or to link to the new last node |
| all values the same | `run([2, 2, 2])` | moving on after a delete and skipping a node |
| a carry at the end | 99 + 1 | the answer has one more digit than either number |

Each task asks you for 1 to 3 tests like these, each a different kind of edge case.

### Time and space of a linked list solution
**Time.** Count the links your code follows, and the links it changes:

| The code | Time |
| --- | --- |
| one walk through the list | O(n) |
| walking to position i | O(i) |
| inserting or deleting after a node you hold | O(1) |
| inserting at the front | O(1) |
| adding at the end, with a tail | O(1) |
| adding at the end, without a tail | O(n), to walk there |
| walking to position i for each of q questions | O(n × q); copying the values first makes it O(n + q) |

**Space.** Count what the code keeps, besides the list it was given:

| The code keeps | Space |
| --- | --- |
| a few pointers, while it changes links in place | O(1) |
| a copy of the values in a Python list | O(n) |
| new nodes for the answer | one per node of the answer |

=== keypoints
- A linked list keeps each item in its own **node**, with a link to the next node. The list keeps the **head**; the last node's `next` is None.
- Reaching item i means following i links from the head: O(i), and O(n) at worst. There's no address formula, so no binary search.
- Inserting or deleting at the front is O(1): `head = Node(x, head)` and `head = head.next`.
- Holding a node, inserting after it (`new = Node(x, node.next)`, then `node.next = new`) or deleting after it (`node.next = node.next.next`) is O(1). Getting to the node is the slow part.
- While walking, stay on a node after deleting the one after it, and step over a node you've inserted. Compare nodes with `is`, not by their values.
- A tail pointer makes adding at the end O(1); without one, every append walks the whole list.
- Singly linked nodes have `next`; doubly linked nodes have `prev` and `next`, so they can walk backwards and delete a node they hold in O(1); a circular list's last node links back to the first.
- Choose a linked list for lots of inserts and deletes at the front or at a place you hold. Choose an array for access by index, binary search and fast walking.
- Patterns: walk with a pointer; build at the front when the answer comes out backwards; change the links after a node; build in order with a tail; and copy the values into a list when you need indexes.
- Combinations: changing links as you walk; building a new list as you walk; building backwards as you walk; reading backwards from a copy; and relinking nodes onto tails.

=== patterns
- walk | Walking with a pointer
- front | Building at the front
- after | Changing the links after a node
- tail | Building in order with a tail
- array | Copying the values into a list
combos:
- walk + after | Changing links as you walk
- walk + tail | Building a new list as you walk
- walk + front | Building backwards as you walk
- array + front | Reading backwards from a copy
- tail + after | Relinking nodes onto tails

=== example: Counting the links
intro: A list built by inserting at the front, then three items fetched by walking, counting the links followed for each one.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def get(head, i):
    node = head
    links = 0
    for step in range(i):
        node = node.next
        links += 1
    return node.val, links

head = None
for x in [50, 40, 30, 20, 10]:
    head = Node(x, head)

for i in [0, 2, 4]:
    print(i, get(head, i))
```
1-4: The node class: a value and a link to the next node.
6-12: Walk i links from the head, counting them, and return the value with the count.
14-16: Start with an empty list (head is None) and insert at the front five times. The last value inserted, 10, ends up first: the list is 10, 20, 30, 40, 50.
18-19: Item i costs i links: O(i). An array would reach any of them in one step.

=== example: Front inserts, array against linked list
intro: Inserting n items at the front, one by one. The array counts how many items it shifts; the linked list counts how many links it changes.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def array_front_inserts(n):
    arr = []
    moves = 0
    for x in range(n):
        moves += len(arr)
        arr.insert(0, x)
    return moves

def linked_front_inserts(n):
    head = None
    changes = 0
    for x in range(n):
        head = Node(x, head)
        changes += 1
    return changes

for n in [10, 100, 1000]:
    print(n, array_front_inserts(n), linked_front_inserts(n))
```
6-12: Each insert at the front of an array shifts every item that's already there: `len(arr)` moves.
14-19: Each insert at the front of a linked list makes one node and moves the head: one change, whatever the length.
21-22: The array makes 0 + 1 + 2 and onwards up to n − 1 moves, which is n(n − 1) / 2, O(n²) in all. The linked list makes n changes, O(n).

=== animation: Walking to item 3
view: linked head ptr=node
intro: There's no formula for where item 3 is. Watch `node` start at the head and follow three links to get there.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(17, Node(42, Node(8, Node(23))))
node = head
for step in range(3):
    node = node.next
print(node.val)
```
1: The node class: a value and a link to the next node.
3: Store this node's value, {val}.
4: Store the link: {'None, so this is the last node' if next is None else 'the node holding ' + str(next.val)}.
6: Build four nodes, from the inside out: 23, then 8, then 42, then 17. head is the first one.
7: Start at the head.
8: Take the next step: 3 steps in all.
9: Follow the link from {node.val} to {node.next.val}.
10: Three links later, node holds item 3: {node.val}. Item i takes i steps: O(i).

=== animation: Inserting after a node
view: linked head ptr=node,new
linked new
intro: We hold the node with 42 and insert 99 right after it. Watch the two links change, in the right order, while every node stays where it is.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(17, Node(42, Node(8)))
node = head.next
new = Node(99)
new.next = node.next
node.next = new
print(head.val, head.next.val, head.next.next.val, head.next.next.next.val)
```
1: The node class: a value and a link to the next node.
3: Store this node's value, {val}.
4: Store the link to the next node.
6: A list of three nodes: 17, 42, 8.
7: We're holding the node with 42.
8: Make the new node. Nothing links to it yet, and it links to nothing.
9: Step 1: the new node links to the node after 42, which holds {node.next.val}.
10: Step 2: 42 links to the new node. Now 99 is in the list.
11: Two links changed and nothing moved: O(1).

