solution:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def is_palindrome(head):
    values = []
    node = head
    while node is not None:
        values.append(node.val)
        node = node.next
    i = 0
    j = len(values) - 1
    while i < j:
        if values[i] != values[j]:
            return False
        i += 1
        j -= 1
    return True


def run(values):
    return is_palindrome(from_list(values))
```
tests:
- run([1, 2, 2, 1])
- run([1, 2])
- run([7])
- run([1, 2, 1])
- run([1, 2, 3, 2, 2])
- run([i % 10 for i in range(10000)] + [5] + [i % 10 for i in range(9999, -1, -1)])
fail[2]: 1 and 2 are different, so the list doesn't read the same backwards.
fail[3]: One node always reads the same both ways.
fail[4]: With an odd length, the middle value has nothing to match: 1, 2, 1 reads the same both ways.
fail[5]: The 1 and the 2 at the two ends already differ.
fail[6]: 20,001 values, mirrored around a 5 in the middle.
edges:
- one node :: len(values) == 1 :: run([4])
- an even length :: len(values) % 2 == 0 :: run([4, 4])
- an odd length above 1 :: len(values) % 2 == 1 and len(values) > 1 :: run([4, 1, 4])
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(n) | space=O(n)
complexity_note: n is the number of nodes.
why_time: One walk to copy, then at most n / 2 comparisons.
why_space: The copy holds all n values.
hint: Walk the list, appending each value to values.
hint: Set `i = 0` and `j = len(values) - 1`. While i < j, return False if `values[i] != values[j]`; then add 1 to i and take 1 from j.
right: Copying the values gives back what a singly linked list doesn't have, the last item and a way to walk backwards, at the cost of O(n) space.
wrong: Copy the values into a Python list, then compare `values[i]` with `values[j]` from both ends towards the middle, returning False at the first difference.

=== task: code easy
title: Remove Duplicates from Sorted List
leetcode: 83 | Remove Duplicates from Sorted List | remove-duplicates-from-sorted-list
patterns: walk + after
prompt: The values of the linked list are sorted, so equal values sit next to each other. Write `delete_duplicates(head)` that deletes nodes so that every value appears once, and returns the head.

Walk with node, and look at the node after it. If `node.next` holds the same value, delete it with `node.next = node.next.next`, and **stay** on node: the new next node may be another copy. Only move on when the next value is different.

**Example 1:** `run([1, 1, 2])` returns `[1, 2]`.

**Example 2:** `run([1, 1, 2, 3, 3])` returns `[1, 2, 3]`.

**Constraints:** the list has 0 to 300 nodes, −100 ≤ every value ≤ 100, and the values are sorted.
starter:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def delete_duplicates(head):
    node = head
    # your code here
    return head


def run(values):
    return to_list(delete_duplicates(from_list(values)))
```
solution:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def delete_duplicates(head):
    node = head
    while node is not None and node.next is not None:
        if node.next.val == node.val:
            node.next = node.next.next
        else:
            node = node.next
    return head


def run(values):
    return to_list(delete_duplicates(from_list(values)))
```
tests:
- run([1, 1, 2])
- run([1, 1, 2, 3, 3])
- run([])
- run([5])
- run([2, 2, 2, 2])
- run([i // 3 - 50 for i in range(300)])
fail[2]: The 1s and the 3s each lose a copy.
fail[3]: An empty list has nothing to delete: check that node isn't None before reading `node.next`.
fail[4]: One node has no duplicates.
fail[5]: Four 2s become one. Moving on after each delete would skip a copy and leave [2, 2].
fail[6]: 300 values from −50 to 49, each three times.
edges:
- an empty list :: len(values) == 0 :: run([])
- a run of three or more :: any(values[i] == values[i + 1] == values[i + 2] for i in range(len(values) - 2)) :: run([3, 3, 3])
- no runs of three :: len(values) > 0 and not any(values[i] == values[i + 1] == values[i + 2] for i in range(len(values) - 2)) :: run([3, 3, 4])
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(n) | space=O(1)
complexity_note: n is the number of nodes.
why_time: Each step either deletes a node or moves on, so there are at most n steps.
why_space: Nodes are unlinked in place; only the pointer is kept.
hint: Loop `while node is not None and node.next is not None:`.
hint: If `node.next.val == node.val`, set `node.next = node.next.next`; otherwise move on with `node = node.next`.
right: Sorted copies are neighbours, so each node only needs to look at the one after it, and staying put after a delete catches a whole run of copies.
wrong: Walk with node: while the next node holds the same value, link past it; when it doesn't, move on. Return head.

=== task: code easy
title: Merge Two Sorted Lists
leetcode: 21 | Merge Two Sorted Lists | merge-two-sorted-lists
patterns: walk + tail
prompt: list1 and list2 are linked lists sorted from smallest to biggest. Write `merge_two_lists(list1, list2)` that links all their nodes into one sorted list and returns its head, using the nodes you have instead of making new ones.

Walk both lists at once. At each step, take whichever front node is smaller (list1's on a tie), move that list's pointer on, and link the node onto the end of the answer with a tail. When one list runs out, the rest of the other is already sorted: link it on in one step.

**Example 1:** `run([1, 2, 4], [1, 3, 4])` returns `[1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 4]`.

**Example 2:** `run([], [0])` returns `[0]`.

**Constraints:** each list has 0 to 50 nodes, −100 ≤ every value ≤ 100, and both lists are sorted.
starter:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def merge_two_lists(list1, list2):
    head = None
    tail = None
    # your code here
    return head


def run(values1, values2):
    return to_list(merge_two_lists(from_list(values1), from_list(values2)))
```
solution:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def merge_two_lists(list1, list2):
    head = None
    tail = None
    while list1 is not None and list2 is not None:
        if list1.val <= list2.val:
            node = list1
            list1 = list1.next
        else:
            node = list2
            list2 = list2.next
        if tail is None:
            head = node
        else:
            tail.next = node
        tail = node
    rest = list1 if list1 is not None else list2
    if tail is None:
        return rest
    tail.next = rest
    return head


def run(values1, values2):
    return to_list(merge_two_lists(from_list(values1), from_list(values2)))
```
tests:
- run([1, 2, 4], [1, 3, 4])
- run([], [])
- run([], [0])
- run([5], [1, 2, 3])
- run([2, 2], [2])
- run([i * 4 - 100 for i in range(50)], [i * 3 - 50 for i in range(50)])
fail[2]: Two empty lists merge into an empty list.
fail[3]: list1 is empty, so the answer is all of list2. Check for an empty answer (tail None) before linking on the rest.
fail[4]: The 5 is left over at the end, after list2 runs out.
fail[5]: Equal values from both lists all stay: three 2s.
fail[6]: Two lists of 50 values each, with some values in both.
edges:
- an empty list :: len(values1) == 0 or len(values2) == 0 :: run([], [1])
- one list all before the other :: len(values1) > 0 and len(values2) > 0 and (max(values1) < min(values2) or max(values2) < min(values1)) :: run([1, 2], [5])
- the lists interleave :: len(values1) > 0 and len(values2) > 0 and max(values1) >= min(values2) and max(values2) >= min(values1) :: run([1, 3], [2])
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(m + n) | space=O(1)
complexity_options: O(m + n); O(m × n); O((m + n) log(m + n))
complexity_note: m and n are the lengths of the two lists.
why_time: Each step moves one node onto the answer, and the leftovers are linked on in one step.
why_space: The nodes are relinked; only head, tail and the two pointers are kept.
hint: While both lists have nodes, pick the smaller front node, `node`, and move that list's pointer to its next node.
hint: Link node on: `head = node` if tail is None, else `tail.next = node`; then `tail = node`.
hint: After the loop, take whichever list is left: if tail is None, return it; otherwise set `tail.next` to it and return head.
right: The smallest node left is always at the front of one of the two lists, so comparing two nodes per step builds the merged list in one pass, and the leftover part is already sorted.
wrong: While both lists have nodes, move the smaller front node onto the end of the answer with a tail. Then link on whatever is left of the other list, and return the head.

=== task: code easy
title: Number to Binary List
patterns: front
prompt: Write `binary_list(n)` that returns a linked list of the binary digits of n, from the most significant digit to the least, with no leading zeros (0 itself is a single 0).

`n % 2` is the last bit, and `n // 2` drops it, so the bits come out last bit first. Insert each one at the front, `head = Node(n % 2, head)`, and the list ends up in the right order without being reversed. The starter makes the first node before the loop, so that 0 still gets its one node.

**Example 1:** `run(5)` returns `[1, 0, 1]`.

**Example 2:** `run(8)` returns `[1, 0, 0, 0]`.

**Constraints:** 0 ≤ n ≤ 10¹⁸.
starter:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def binary_list(n):
    head = Node(n % 2)
    n //= 2
    # your code here
    return head


def run(n):
    return to_list(binary_list(n))
```
solution:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def binary_list(n):
    head = Node(n % 2)
    n //= 2
    while n > 0:
        head = Node(n % 2, head)
        n //= 2
    return head


def run(n):
    return to_list(binary_list(n))
```
tests:
- run(5)
- run(0)
- run(1)
- run(8)
- run(255)
- run(10 ** 18)
fail[2]: 0 is a single 0.
fail[3]: 1 is a single 1.
fail[4]: 8 is 1000: the 1 comes out last and goes in front of the three 0s.
fail[5]: 255 is eight 1s.
fail[6]: 10¹⁸ has 60 bits.
edges:
- zero :: n == 0 :: run(0)
- an odd number :: n % 2 == 1 :: run(3)
- an even number above 0 :: n > 0 and n % 2 == 0 :: run(6)
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(log n) | space=O(log n)
complexity_note: n is the number; it has about log₂ n bits.
why_time: Each step halves n.
why_space: The list has one node per bit.
hint: The node made before the loop holds the last bit; every node made in the loop goes in front of it.
hint: Loop `while n > 0:`, setting `head = Node(n % 2, head)` and then `n //= 2`.
right: The bits come out in reverse, and inserting at the front reverses them again, so the list is in order with no extra pass.
wrong: Start with a node for `n % 2` and halve n. While n is above 0, insert `n % 2` at the front and halve n again.

=== task: code easy
title: Expand the Runs
patterns: tail
prompt: pairs is a list of `[value, count]` pairs. Write `expand_runs(pairs)` that returns a linked list in which each value appears count times in a row, in the order of the pairs.

Build the list from the start to the end with a tail: make each node, link it after tail (or make it the head if there's no tail yet), and move tail to it. Walking to the end of the list for every new node would make the whole thing O(n²).

**Example 1:** `run([[3, 2], [5, 1]])` returns `[3, 3, 5]`.

**Example 2:** `run([])` returns `[]`.

**Constraints:** at most 1000 pairs, 1 ≤ every count, the counts add up to at most 10⁴, and −10⁹ ≤ every value ≤ 10⁹.
starter:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def expand_runs(pairs):
    head = None
    tail = None
    # your code here
    return head


def run(pairs):
    return to_list(expand_runs(pairs))
```
solution:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def expand_runs(pairs):
    head = None
    tail = None
    for value, count in pairs:
        for _ in range(count):
            node = Node(value)
            if tail is None:
                head = node
            else:
                tail.next = node
            tail = node
    return head


def run(pairs):
    return to_list(expand_runs(pairs))
```
tests:
- run([[3, 2], [5, 1]])
- run([])
- run([[7, 1]])
- run([[1, 3], [1, 2]])
- run([[-2, 2], [0, 1], [4, 2]])
- (lambda t: [len(t), t[:6], t[-6:], sum(t)])(run([[i, i % 5 + 1] for i in range(1000)]))
fail[2]: No pairs: an empty list, with head None.
fail[3]: One value, once.
fail[4]: Two runs of the same value make one long run of five 1s.
fail[5]: −2 twice, 0 once, then 4 twice.
fail[6]: 1,000 pairs making 3,000 nodes. Walking to the end for each new node is far too slow; keep a tail.
edges:
- no pairs :: len(pairs) == 0 :: run([])
- every count 1 :: len(pairs) > 0 and all(c == 1 for v, c in pairs) :: run([[4, 1], [2, 1]])
- a count above 1 :: any(c > 1 for v, c in pairs) :: run([[4, 2]])
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(n) | space=O(n)
complexity_note: n is the number of nodes made, the total of the counts.
why_time: Each node is made and linked in O(1).
why_space: The answer has n nodes.
hint: Loop `for value, count in pairs:` and, inside it, `for _ in range(count):`.
hint: Make `node = Node(value)`. If tail is None, set head to it, else `tail.next = node`; then `tail = node`.
right: The tail is always the last node, so each new node is linked in one step, and the list is built in order in O(n).
wrong: For each pair, make count nodes. Link each one after the tail (or make it the head if there's no tail yet), and move the tail to it.

=== task: code easy
title: Look Up Many Indexes
patterns: array
prompt: Write `values_at(head, indexes)` that returns a list of the values at the given indexes of the linked list, in the order of indexes. Index 0 is the head, and every index is inside the list.

Walking from the head for each index costs O(i) per index, which adds up when there are thousands of them. Copy the values into a Python list in one walk instead; then each index is a single O(1) read.

**Example 1:** `run([5, 8, 2], [2, 0])` returns `[2, 5]`.

**Example 2:** `run([7], [0, 0, 0])` returns `[7, 7, 7]`.

**Constraints:** the list has 1 to 10⁴ nodes, and there are at most 10⁴ indexes.
starter:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def values_at(head, indexes):
    values = []
    # your code here
    return []


def run(values, indexes):
    return values_at(from_list(values), indexes)
```
solution:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def values_at(head, indexes):
    values = []
    node = head
    while node is not None:
        values.append(node.val)
        node = node.next
    return [values[i] for i in indexes]


def run(values, indexes):
    return values_at(from_list(values), indexes)
```
tests:
- run([5, 8, 2], [2, 0])
- run([7], [0, 0, 0])
- run([1, 2, 3, 4], [])
- run([4, 4, 9], [1, 2])
- run([10, 20, 30, 40, 50], [4, 3, 2, 1, 0])
- (lambda t: [len(t), t[:6], sum(t)])(run(list(range(5000)), [i * 7 % 5000 for i in range(5000)]))
fail[2]: The same index three times.
fail[3]: No indexes: an empty answer.
fail[4]: Index 1 is the second 4, and index 2 is the 9.
fail[5]: All five indexes, backwards.
fail[6]: 5,000 nodes and 5,000 indexes. Walking from the head for each index is far too slow; copy the values once.
edges:
- no indexes :: len(indexes) == 0 :: run([1, 2], [])
- an index more than once :: len(set(indexes)) < len(indexes) :: run([1, 2], [1, 1])
- every index once :: len(indexes) > 0 and len(set(indexes)) == len(indexes) :: run([1, 2], [1, 0])
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(n + q) | space=O(n)
complexity_options: O(n + q); O(n × q); O(q)
complexity_note: n is the number of nodes and q the number of indexes.
why_time: One walk to copy, then O(1) per index.
why_space: The copy holds all n values.
hint: Walk the list, appending each value to values.
hint: Return `[values[i] for i in indexes]`.
right: The copy turns "walk i links" into "read item i", so the cost of the walk is paid once instead of for every index.
wrong: Copy the values into a Python list in one walk, then read `values[i]` for each index.

=== task: code easy
title: Remove Every Second Node
patterns: walk + after
prompt: Write `remove_every_second(head)` that deletes the 2nd node, the 4th, the 6th and onwards (the nodes at the odd indexes 1, 3, 5 and onwards), keeps the others, and returns the head.

Walk with node on the nodes you keep. Delete the node after it with `node.next = node.next.next`, then move on to the node that is now next, which is the next one to keep. Stop when node, or the node after it, is None.

**Example 1:** `run([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])` returns `[1, 3, 5]`.

**Example 2:** `run([1, 2, 3, 4])` returns `[1, 3]`.

**Constraints:** the list has 0 to 10⁴ nodes.
starter:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
