fail[3]: 0 × 2 = 0.
fail[4]: 5 × 2 = 10: one digit becomes two.
fail[5]: 49 × 2 = 98: the 4 becomes 8, plus 1 because the 9 after it is 5 or more.
fail[6]: A 10,000-digit number that starts with a 7, so the answer has 10,001 digits.
edges:
- one digit :: len(digits) == 1 :: run([3])
- a longer number, head 5 or more :: len(digits) > 1 and digits[0] >= 5 :: run([6, 1])
- a longer number, head under 5 :: len(digits) > 1 and digits[0] < 5 :: run([2, 7])
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(n) | space=O(1)
complexity_note: n is the number of digits; at most one new node is made.
why_time: One walk, O(1) per digit.
why_space: The digits change in place, and at most one node is added.
hint: If `head.val >= 5`, set `head = Node(0, head)`.
hint: Walk with node: `node.val = node.val * 2 % 10`, then add 1 if `node.next` isn't None and `node.next.val >= 5`.
right: Whether a digit gets a carry depends only on the digit after it, so one forward walk can finish each digit, and a node inserted at the front makes room for the only carry that can leave the number.
wrong: If the first digit is 5 or more, insert a 0 at the front. Then walk the list, setting each digit to twice itself mod 10, plus 1 if the next digit is 5 or more.

